<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.7.4: Writing Consistent and Inconsistent Systems of Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00473</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.7.4: Writing Consistent and Inconsistent Systems of Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a1e97352-cdf9-4825-b006-c5839ac131d4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Here is an equation: \( 5x-2y=10 \).</para>
<para>Create a second equation that would make a system of equations with:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item>One solution</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Compare Your Answer.  Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.  </para>
<para>\(3x - y = 7\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>No solutions</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Compare Your Answer.  Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.  </para>
<para>\(5x - 2y = 20\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Infinitely many solutions</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Compare Your Answer.  Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.  </para>
<para>\(15x - 6y = 30\)</para>

<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>In Activity 2.13.4 and Activity 2.14.1 (graph B), systems of inequalities with no solutions are shown, and these will provide another opportunity to discuss when a system has no solutions and how to tell.</para></section>
</content>
